  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule are both in place, and the rule is enabled.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The `kibana_sample_data_logs` index has about 14,000 web requests. The time field is `timestamp`. Each request records:\n- the response code (`response.keyword`)\n- `bytes`\n- the client IP (`clientip`)\n- source and destination country (`geo.src` / `geo.dest`)\n- `host`, `url`, the file `extension` and `machine.os`\n\nOnly three response codes appear: 200 (12,832 requests), 404 (801) and 503 (441). The timestamps run from 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03, so much of the data is dated in the future.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-2cd56b85 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-2cd56b85-web-logs-overview-a1ea7a9a0b68\n- **Single numbers:** total requests, unique visitors (counted by client IP), and total bytes served.\n- **Requests over time:** a bar chart split by response code.\n- **Response code share:** a pie chart.\n- **Top 10 source countries:** a bar chart.\n- **Top 10 requested URLs:** a table with request counts and bytes.\n\nBecause the data runs into the future, I set the dashboard's default time range to the last 7 days through 60 days ahead (`now-7d` \u2192 `now+60d`) so it covers the data.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-2cd56b85 5xx spike\"** (id `36015c3c-0805-4c29-bbf7-5f9a37b193ef`) is enabled.\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" by timestamp, and fires when there are more than 10 in the last 5 minutes.\n- I checked the rule's search against the full index: it matches 441 requests, which are exactly the 503s.\n- No notification is attached, so a firing alert only shows up in Kibana.",
